1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,602
Think you can catch pink eye just by looking at someone?

2
00:00:02,922 --> 00:00:09,119
That's a myth — but it spreads faster than most people realize, through direct contact with discharge from an infected eye.

3
00:00:09,439 --> 00:00:16,173
Bacteria or viruses reaching the conjunctiva — the membrane covering your eye — cause the redness, itching, and discharge.

4
00:00:16,493 --> 00:00:23,200
First, touching your eyes with unwashed hands keeps the infection circulating — discharge on your fingers goes right back in.

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:26,565
Second, sleeping in contact lenses raises your risk.

6
00:00:26,885 --> 00:00:34,289
A closed eyelid traps bacteria against the cornea overnight, and rinsing lenses in tap water instead of solution adds more risk.

7
00:00:34,609 --> 00:00:43,542
Finally, sharing towels or pillowcases with someone infected makes transmission far more likely — the virus survives on fabric long enough to pass along.

8
00:00:43,862 --> 00:00:52,876
Wash your hands before touching your eyes, remove contacts before bed and clean them with solution, and keep towels and pillowcases to yourself until symptoms clear.

9
00:00:53,196 --> 00:00:59,071
See a doctor if swelling is severe or your vision changes — this is general info, not a diagnosis.
